  "structure": {
    "block_count": 44,
    "blocks": [
      {
        "block_id": "block_000000",
        "block_index": 0,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h1",
        "text": "Why Your Toddler Is Suddenly So Bossy — and What You Can Do About It",
        "text_sha256": "ad5d7ff1e2b72983040de3b70ff8bc1566ed94a1e80dac4bc2460ad150fb7e41",
        "word_count": 15
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000001",
        "block_index": 1,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Light copy updates and updates to sources, fact-checked for accuracy.",
        "text_sha256": "eba5039f45c1916cc2389453731fcc7b2573a3e013f9c6efa39048dc58a6b4f0",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000002",
        "block_index": 2,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Toddler Behavior",
        "text_sha256": "18e4b9b668548d07c804fb935366c13931211d0a372019470dff77b378ce9417",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000003",
        "block_index": 3,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your little tyrant orders everyone around! Here's how to tone down those domineering ways without dampening her confidence.",
        "text_sha256": "ffbc42a3665cc5436868ccdde581730e5e5a62b01b70effc541bc98a7291bfe8",
        "word_count": 18
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000004",
        "block_index": 4,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Many toddlers want things done their way and on their timetable. It's cute at first, but can get tiring (for you — and for any little friends).",
        "text_sha256": "60ee76c70ddbe3b995c7d6185139a692086e532c63dc5642c4470889324503ae",
        "word_count": 27
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000005",
        "block_index": 5,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "\"What do I do? My girl is 28 months and is SO bossy, to the point that she will even tell us how to sit,\" says What to Expect Community parent CheetoDorito. \"She wants control of every little thing (which I get, since she's 2), but it makes it so hard because she cannot function unless she’s in full control!\"",
        "text_sha256": "484f7a805de90be76076f286581fbfed26c00a12a4d5a73904315fab25b8d647",
        "word_count": 60
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000006",
        "block_index": 6,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Here's how to curb the seemingly endless stream of demands coming from your cutie.",
        "text_sha256": "77b48945261b75276432af5ab42605bb0bf64511619e6cc8fba328ce6275c729",
        "word_count": 14
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000007",
        "block_index": 7,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Key Takeaways",
        "text_sha256": "cc3f7f0f4513969e418b56fb93ec0876fd96dd6498395b04301f70dbddff8a45",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000008",
        "block_index": 8,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Bossiness is common in 2- and 3-year-olds because they’re asserting independence, seeking control in a big-person world, and are still too young to fully understand others’ perspectives — a phase most outgrow after age 3.",
        "text_sha256": "c9df97571c792f114eae1a4f1f95a8461985b011825fb38718c27daddf8d8bb7",
        "word_count": 35
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000009",
        "block_index": 9,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Giving toddlers structured control and positive attention helps reduce bossiness — offering choices, involving them in simple chores, modeling polite behavior, and teaching early social skills like “please,” sharing, and taking turns.",
        "text_sha256": "ae56f4642cc0d3141defbd4e42c6c7ec9a07350fa246d21214535921eeb4a47a",
        "word_count": 32
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000010",
        "block_index": 10,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Set calm, consistent boundaries by deescalating power struggles, correcting demanding behavior, and using time-outs or time-ins when needed to help them reset and learn appropriate ways to communicate.",
        "text_sha256": "8068ae02752abf1cfdc13abe32ae0fdc82d454a070b3e7c09dad965914cc6dba",
        "word_count": 28
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000011",
        "block_index": 11,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Why are toddlers bossy?",
        "text_sha256": "ea78fd72cfd970861ea9f5ccb39864096d03cf420b51f781ebcc89300c461dc2",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000012",
        "block_index": 12,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Bossy behavior is very common among 2- and 3-year-olds. That's because at this age, toddlers love to flex their independence (and unsolicited leadership skills). Trusted SourceNational Institutes of HealthToddler DevelopmentSee All Sources [1]",
        "text_sha256": "8712ab1dc054c69ad8ef0b080c638cf483bc824e8d8f311c40200221734743f9",
        "word_count": 33
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000013",
        "block_index": 13,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Trusted SourceNational Institutes of HealthToddler DevelopmentSee All Sources",
        "text_sha256": "949acbab3bc58e48e703d43ee23d7fde743e3ec1a21c08b21cdd4ee840792c03",
        "word_count": 8
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000014",
        "block_index": 14,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "But when she's 2 years old, your toddler can only see the world as it relates to her, which means that everyone else wants exactly what she wants … right? Is it bossy behavior if she's just guiding you to do what will make the universe (aka her) happy?",
        "text_sha256": "a0aabeaca1ee455e41daec2cd12c5266762ae111ce82cb861387c47790be76c8",
        "word_count": 49
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000015",
        "block_index": 15,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "A bossy child could also be trying to establish her place in a world where grown-ups seem to have all the power. (Mom might make me wear shoes — but I'm in charge of which pair!) And of course, some children may be born with naturally dominant personalities, which can come off as bossiness.",
        "text_sha256": "c55f6eeae939a614ee69c65a6eafca00d35e379bf65ea536648815ecedb576c8",
        "word_count": 54
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000016",
        "block_index": 16,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Fortunately, most little dictators outgrow this phase after age 3 as they become more aware of others' feelings and begin to understand that different people have different wants and needs. Trusted SourceAmerican Academy of PediatricsSocial Development in PreschoolersSee All Sources [2] They'll also start picking up on the fact that other kids really don't dig being told what to do.",
        "text_sha256": "a291801c1f6a0660d68f6bd973d113d99656c3436b6dd6fbc4d151154b2ac6a0",
        "word_count": 60
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000017",
        "block_index": 17,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Trusted SourceAmerican Academy of PediatricsSocial Development in PreschoolersSee All Sources",
        "text_sha256": "699ac0d9f7bc647cca446bb10742ca052e82c79ce8cb91030ec69951b8f2d7c5",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000018",
        "block_index": 18,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "In the meantime, there are many ways to help temper those imperious impulses without discouraging your toddler's innate desire to lead.",
        "text_sha256": "a858990b3f2e9be14a5f24e043baf0120364ac3cc12b8d23d04f009e24ffb887",
        "word_count": 21
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000019",
        "block_index": 19,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "How to handle toddler bossiness",
        "text_sha256": "a3aba2e0b2440fda248475c5e09d0092fa77af55633a584f11433a24880ad8da",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000020",
        "block_index": 20,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "As your tot wises up on her social skills, here's what you can do to guide her toward a less demanding demeanor.",
        "text_sha256": "c396461e058c8741c1681ba3bfdc0876bb9e30b268bc437582b7bde84e05a23f",
        "word_count": 22
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000021",
        "block_index": 21,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Deescalate",
        "text_sha256": "47ec8308dcd2b66f2fd6231e147bf114680c15756c205f65c83905a2f81fb652",
        "word_count": 1
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000022",
        "block_index": 22,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Being bossed around by a 3-year-old can be frustrating, but don't go to war with your toddler. A bossy 3-year-old definitely isn't developmentally capable of plotting out a power struggle.",
        "text_sha256": "49e3f34b7693c885cde1da1e02933ea5ef19751c5d502c643359517322247aae",
        "word_count": 30
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000023",
        "block_index": 23,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Of course, toddlers need to learn the limits of appropriate and safe behavior, but the emphasis should be on learning, not establishing who's king of the mountain.",
        "text_sha256": "f98c3ba781fd605e788a342772be4b150184a8b2ebb35b305ace85ac6b2022c7",
        "word_count": 27
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000024",
        "block_index": 24,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Give back some control",
        "text_sha256": "3b2f0135bf12688fdf5c8fcc7c77bce57806f4c9c93363d66f79e16e7a82ac8c",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000025",
        "block_index": 25,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Sometimes bossiness is just a symptom of feeling out of control. Toddlers aren't at the helm of many of the decisions in their lives. But offering your child choices (\"Do you want to wear the pink skirt or purple one?\") can make her feel empowered.",
        "text_sha256": "b3f9a9ae82c6132bbf3561068ea1587c284fb8f6d0dd5d5bb8de52b1f0e23668",
        "word_count": 45
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000026",
        "block_index": 26,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Boost self-esteem",
        "text_sha256": "ce03897721cd3bc79268a1f48b81535b6f442bbabd1e4f887d698cf4c017adf0",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000027",
        "block_index": 27,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Ask for help with simple chores like putting laundry in the hamper or tearing lettuce for a salad, and offer lots of praise when the task is complete. That way, your child feels important without having to order someone else around.",
        "text_sha256": "0cfa637d74ddbd5f862865b148f65c441e2d3064cabd199cd8640e03b7166d3e",
        "word_count": 41
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000028",
        "block_index": 28,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "This is also a great way to shower attention on your young one while teaching her how to be a good citizen of the house. Sometimes bossy behavior is really just a demand for attention.",
        "text_sha256": "c98c40045ae1be9f4dfab3a8a7d5598fa64cec9fe78f0dd5a778eff9586ce975",
        "word_count": 35
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000029",
        "block_index": 29,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Reinforce social skills",
        "text_sha256": "b248bba52743e38477fa3fb1647f6f811ac0dd0287548b85de736df72936de7c",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000030",
        "block_index": 30,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Make \"please\" a part of your child's vocabulary and teach her how to share and take turns . The playground, a communal box of crayons, and sibling time are all perfect laboratories for learning social skills .",
        "text_sha256": "76f9d19cbbb42b134510db3a519302c4ff07574e2f4f01fcd16d57c47579b526",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000031",
        "block_index": 31,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Encourage empathy for others (\"How would you feel if Susie grabbed your doll?\") when age-appropriate. A child under 3 can't yet understand that line of thinking. Trusted SourceAmerican Academy of PediatricsSocial Development: 2 Year OldsSee All Sources [3]",
        "text_sha256": "9fd94b7c71b1b06603b84ba12c8c2c192d34f9ee6f33f23e5704b6ea93af1960",
        "word_count": 38
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000032",
        "block_index": 32,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Trusted SourceAmerican Academy of PediatricsSocial Development: 2 Year OldsSee All Sources",
        "text_sha256": "31337c721dadc49b83622be57a92088600a0b9926b2729bd0037ccd9b7f97ddb",
        "word_count": 11
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000033",
        "block_index": 33,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Lead by example",
        "text_sha256": "aaed9d4408937a4d255826b6df48f2c1bf8a1dce1cb5608f2dccd69e739ed1d6",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000034",
        "block_index": 34,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Though it may seem like your toddler can magically turn off her ears whenever you give her instructions, she is actually paying very close attention to your behavior. You might even catch her passing on your seemingly unheard instructions with the exact tone and mannerisms to the family pets .",
        "text_sha256": "134abd4f78144419feaf8e55a9d8097e8bfd924fc6f44c3f30e90e5169383f8c",
        "word_count": 50
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000035",
        "block_index": 35,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Model the behavior you want to see and treat your toddler the way you'd like to be treated.",
        "text_sha256": "43408e3d4c262f14b6be88f3899ae85e258d4de41b2aea851a27dc932dd03005",
        "word_count": 18
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000036",
        "block_index": 36,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Set boundaries",
        "text_sha256": "3b3b0acb027d1f41de140919fbf329624039385dccec16471c7e81726dbdff98",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000037",
        "block_index": 37,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "One of the many ironies of toddlerhood: Toddlers crave routine and structure, but seem to like disrupting the status quo too.",
        "text_sha256": "392a3b6047acea9cf73da1c3b43de16086627cc8ab31b64699c13f300dde6f40",
        "word_count": 21
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000038",
        "block_index": 38,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Don't be a doormat. Part of what toddlers are learning each day is how to make reasonable requests. When your child's demands or tone of voice are out of bounds, tell her that calmly rather than letting her dictate what you do.",
        "text_sha256": "4626e0f2e1468d195628c2947190853f6f934397ce6aa73ab863dde51c434982",
        "word_count": 42
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000039",
        "block_index": 39,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Want more tips on wrangling your toddler through the ups and downs? The What to Expect app has you covered, with expert-backed articles and ways to connect with other parents who get it.",
        "text_sha256": "3fdf7c743a4ce5ec385ba54071afc8e863419c43d83e8eb3b5e61b1c5ac9087e",
        "word_count": 33
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000040",
        "block_index": 40,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Give a time out (or a time-in)",
        "text_sha256": "3bb6b37bcf5cd3deb476d4b285a02bcde1cbca222e7a5b189ac80a5e4beb8a99",
        "word_count": 7
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000041",
        "block_index": 41,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "It's important to give your child an opportunity to fix her behavior before giving her a time-out. But sometimes, the only way to stop bossy behavior in its tracks is to remove your child from the situation.",
        "text_sha256": "774c58bfc4fd94d95d53e53b010110c99c76facaab2d870192af2116971b1a37",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000042",
        "block_index": 42,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "A time-out, when used appropriately, can help your toddler reset. Generally, it should last about one minute for each year of life (e.g., three minutes for a 3-year-old).",
        "text_sha256": "e4835ade34af3e64e6b181023801c8b977a6391e408475f9f4246017983122c1",
        "word_count": 28
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000043",
        "block_index": 43,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "You can also try a modified version called a time-in . A time-in is a brief, close, positive interaction with a parent or caregiver. Time-ins — going with your child to a quiet spot — can allow a child to cool off with you, so you can talk about what happened and how it could be done better the next time.",
        "text_sha256": "496adb93314e386ded58df3b5d4b7c65662b463a689342afc7d39817085ff6bc",
        "word_count": 61
      }
    ],
    "content_word_count": 1085,
    "heading_count": 11
  },
  "title": "Why Your Toddler Is Suddenly So Bossy — and What You Can Do About It",
